<LanguageData>

  <!-- Weapon labels follow Odyssey's own Brazilian Portuguese uniques ("arco grande único",
       "fuzil de assalto único"): the adjective postposes and, unlike French's invariant "unique",
       "único" AGREES in gender. Our roster is 4 feminine (espada longa, lança, maça, faca) and
       3 masculine (gládio, machado, martelo de guerra), so each label is inflected per weapon and
       the description's "forjados"/"forjadas" agrees with it too.

       namerLabels are bare lowercase nouns with NO gender marker, matching Odyssey's pt-BR namer,
       which preposes every adjective slot onto [weapon_type] and so can never agree with it. The
       nouns come from Core's own pt-BR weapon-noun corpus (Strings/Words/Nouns/Weapons.txt: adaga,
       clava, cutelo, espada, faca, gládio, lâmina, lança, machado, marreta, martelo, pique,
       porrete), plus "gume" (cutting edge) and "azagaia" (light spear) where that corpus has no
       distinct word. Note "clava" renders bludgeon, never mace: mace stays Core's "maça". -->

